For an existing LLM, approximate unlearning fine-tunes the trained model for a small number of steps. The standard setup has three ingredients:

- the **target model**, already trained on everything;
- a **forget set** $D_f$ to remove and a **retain set** $D_r$ of data whose behavior must be kept;
- an ideal **retrained model** (also called the retain model), trained only without $D_f$, used in research as the reference.

The goal is lesson 2's: a model that looks as if it were retrained without $D_f$. No LLM method certifies that, so here it is checked empirically, against the retrained model.

The obvious first idea is to run training backwards on the forget set.

How the sign comes out, with MUSE's NEWS numbers. MUSE orients the AUC so that the target model, which knows the forget articles, scores near 0 (its forget texts look far *more* familiar than held-out ones), while the retrained model scores 0.47, close to chance. PrivLeak is the difference divided by the retrained AUC:

- target model, AUC about 0.001: $(0.001 - 0.47)/0.47 \approx -100\%$ (MUSE reports -99.8);
- an over-unlearned model with AUC 0.978: $(0.978 - 0.47)/0.47 = +108\%$, the value MUSE reports for gradient ascent with a retain loss (108.1);
- a model with AUC 0.47: PrivLeak 0, membership as hidden as after retraining.

Their findings: WHP generalized well under its own familiarity metric, but

- more Harry Potter knowledge than baseline could reliably be extracted with jailbreaks and in-context summaries;
- on trivia questions WHP performed about as well as the original Llama-2 model, and even better on short-answer questions;
- probes on hidden states recovered the correct answers about as accurately as on the original model for easy questions, with a small gap on hard ones;
- there was collateral unlearning in related domains.

In other words, the generic-label fine-tuning mostly changed how the model *writes* about Harry Potter, not what it represents.

Łucki et al. (2024) pushed further with *adaptive* white-box attacks, designed against the specific defense, on the public RMU checkpoint of Zephyr-7B, and on NPO and a DPO refusal-training baseline they trained themselves. Before unlearning the model scored 64.4% on WMDP-Bio; RMU brought it to 29.9% (chance is 25%). Then:

- fine-tuning on **10 examples from the retain set**, which by construction hold none of the forget knowledge, brought RMU back to 61.6% (NPO 52.7%, DPO 57.0%) at under 2 points of MMLU cost;
- fine-tuning on 5 forget-set examples brought RMU to 62.4%;
- a universal adversarial prefix from an enhanced GCG search (an automated token-by-token prompt optimizer) reached 53.9%, and pruning a small set of weights 54.0%;
- removing per-layer directions from the activations reached 64.7%, above the original model.

A reporting checklist for any unlearning result:

- forget metrics *and* utility, read together at the chosen stopping point;
- the retrained or retain model as the reference, never zero;
- forget quality on paraphrased and perturbed answers, not only loss on the training text;
- the quantized model users will actually run;
- a relearning test: a few steps on unrelated or related data;
- for open weights, adaptive attacks from lesson 6.
